@@ -0,0 +1,360 @@
+Agents
+These agent definitions will be passed to an expert who will convert each into a working autonomous agent with the described responsibilities, inputs, outputs, and behaviors.
+
+Analyzer
+Scans a large diff to identify distinct logical themes and maps each theme to its relevant files, producing a structured breakdown for downstream agents.
+∙ Parse the full diff and list all modified files with change statistics
+∙ Identify logical groupings: bugfix, feature, refactor, dependency update, config change
+∙ Assign each file to one or more candidate themes
+∙ Flag files that span multiple themes as potential split points
+∙ Estimate complexity per theme (small/medium/large based on lines and files)
+∙ Output a structured list of themes with names, purposes, and file mappings
+∙ Note any obvious dependencies between identified themes
+∙ Surface ambiguous changes that need human clarification
+
+Extractor
+Isolates changes for a single theme from the larger diff, producing a standalone patch that contains only the relevant hunks and files.
+∙ Accept a theme definition from Analyzer with its file list
+∙ Extract only the hunks relevant to that specific theme
+∙ Handle files that contain changes for multiple themes by splitting hunks
+∙ Preserve original commit attribution where possible
+∙ Generate a clean git-format-patch output
+∙ Document any hunks that couldn’t be cleanly separated
+∙ Flag missing dependencies that exist in other themes
+∙ Output a patch file and a list of unresolved cross-theme references
+
+Fixer
+Takes an extracted patch and ensures it compiles, passes linting, and runs tests independently without relying on other split patches.
+∙ Apply the patch to a clean branch from upstream base
+∙ Run the build process and capture any failures
+∙ Add minimal import statements or type definitions if missing
+∙ Stub or mock dependencies that live in other split PRs
+∙ Run the linter and auto-fix what’s possible
+∙ Execute the test suite relevant to changed files
+∙ Never expand functionality beyond what the patch originally did
+∙ Output a working patch with a pass/fail report
+
+Scoper
+Evaluates an extracted patch to determine if it’s appropriately sized and focused, rejecting patches that are too large or do more than one thing.
+∙ Receive a patch from Extractor or Fixer
+∙ Count lines changed, files touched, and logical concerns addressed
+∙ Reject if more than one distinct behavior is being modified
+∙ Reject if size exceeds reasonable single-PR thresholds
+∙ Provide specific feedback on what should be split further
+∙ Approve patches that pass focus and size checks
+∙ Return rejected patches to Extractor with splitting guidance
+∙ Track rejection history to prevent infinite loops
+
+Writer
+Crafts the PR title, description, commit messages, and metadata for a single themed patch following upstream conventions.
+∙ Accept a working patch and its theme metadata
+∙ Write a title under 50 characters in imperative mood
+∙ Compose a description covering what, why, and how
+∙ Structure commit messages to tell a coherent story
+∙ Reference the original source PR or issue
+∙ Suggest appropriate labels and reviewers
+∙ Follow the target repository’s contribution guidelines
+∙ Output a complete draft ready for submission
+
+Verifier
+Checks that the code in a patch actually implements what the theme claims it does, catching mismatches between intent and implementation.
+∙ Read the theme definition and its stated purpose
+∙ Analyze the actual code changes in the patch
+∙ Confirm the code achieves the described goal
+∙ Detect dead code or incomplete implementations
+∙ Flag logic that contradicts the stated intent
+∙ Verify edge cases mentioned in the theme are handled
+∙ Pass patches that do what they claim
+∙ Reject with specifics when implementation doesn’t match intent
+
+Aligner
+Ensures the PR description accurately reflects what the code actually does, catching drift between documentation and implementation.
+∙ Compare the Writer’s PR description against actual patch contents
+∙ Flag claims in the description not supported by code
+∙ Flag code changes not mentioned in the description
+∙ Check that the title accurately summarizes the change
+∙ Verify commit messages align with their actual diffs
+∙ Suggest description edits to close any gaps
+∙ Approve when documentation and code match
+∙ Reject with specific mismatches to fix
+
+Observer
+Compares the complete set of split PRs against the original diff to ensure nothing was lost, added, or fundamentally changed in approach.
+∙ Hold the original diff as ground truth
+∙ Collect all finalized split patches
+∙ Verify combined coverage equals the original
+∙ Detect any logic that was added during splitting
+∙ Detect any logic that was lost during splitting
+∙ Flag changes in approach or implementation strategy
+∙ Confirm the splits are a pure decomposition
+∙ Approve the set or reject with drift specifics
+
+Sequencer
+Analyzes dependencies between split PRs and determines the optimal order for submission to maximize merge likelihood.
+∙ Receive all approved split PRs
+∙ Build a dependency graph based on code references
+∙ Identify PRs that can be submitted in parallel
+∙ Order dependent PRs for sequential submission
+∙ Place refactors before features that use them
+∙ Place dependency updates before code that requires them
+∙ Output an ordered list or DAG for submission
+∙ Note any circular dependencies that need resolution
+
+Skills
+These skill definitions will be passed to an expert who will implement each as a reusable capability that agents can invoke to perform specific technical tasks.
+
+Diff Reading
+Parse and interpret git diffs to understand what changed at the file and hunk level.
+∙ Parse unified diff format into structured data
+∙ Extract file paths, line numbers, and change types
+∙ Separate additions, deletions, and context lines
+∙ Handle binary file indicators
+∙ Parse diff stats for quick size assessment
+∙ Support both single-file and multi-file diffs
+∙ Recognize rename and move operations
+∙ Output structured hunk objects for further processing
+
+Code Semantics
+Understand what code actually does beyond surface-level text changes, grasping behavior and intent.
+∙ Identify function and method boundaries
+∙ Track variable usage and data flow
+∙ Recognize common patterns: guard clauses, factory methods, etc.
+∙ Understand type relationships and inheritance
+∙ Detect side effects and state mutations
+∙ Distinguish structural changes from behavioral changes
+∙ Map imports and external dependencies
+∙ Summarize the purpose of a code block in plain language
+
+Theme Detection
+Group related changes by their logical purpose, distinguishing bugfixes from features from refactors.
+∙ Recognize bugfix patterns: null checks, boundary fixes, error handling
+∙ Recognize feature patterns: new methods, new files, new tests
+∙ Recognize refactor patterns: renames, extractions, reorganization
+∙ Recognize dependency changes: Gemfile, package.json, lock files
+∙ Recognize config changes: environment, CI, linter rules
+∙ Cluster related changes even across distant files
+∙ Assign confidence scores to theme classifications
+∙ Handle ambiguous changes that could fit multiple themes
+
+Git Operations
+Execute git commands to manipulate branches, patches, and history for extraction and validation.
+∙ Create branches from specific refs
+∙ Apply patches with git apply or git am
+∙ Generate patches with git format-patch
+∙ Cherry-pick specific commits
+∙ Handle merge conflicts programmatically
+∙ Reset and clean working directories
+∙ Track patch application success and failure
+∙ Manage multiple worktrees for parallel validation
+
+Dependency Mapping
+Track what code depends on what other code to understand required ordering and isolation challenges.
+∙ Parse import and require statements
+∙ Build a graph of file dependencies
+∙ Identify shared dependencies between themes
+∙ Detect circular dependencies
+∙ Find the minimal set of files needed for a change
+∙ Track runtime vs build-time dependencies
+∙ Map test files to their implementation files
+∙ Output dependency information for Sequencer
+
+Test Execution
+Run test suites and interpret results to validate that patches work correctly in isolation.
+∙ Detect the project’s test framework
+∙ Run full suite or targeted tests by file
+∙ Parse test output for pass/fail/skip counts
+∙ Extract failure messages and stack traces
+∙ Measure test coverage on changed lines
+∙ Handle test timeouts gracefully
+∙ Report flaky test detection
+∙ Output structured test results
+
+Build Validation
+Compile, lint, and type-check code to ensure patches don’t break the build.
+∙ Detect the project’s build system
+∙ Run compilation and capture errors
+∙ Run linter with project configuration
+∙ Run type-checker if applicable
+∙ Parse error output into structured issues
+∙ Distinguish errors from warnings
+∙ Auto-fix linter issues where safe
+∙ Output build status with specific failures
+
+Size Estimation
+Judge whether a change is appropriately scoped for a single PR based on lines, files, and complexity.
+∙ Count lines added, removed, and modified
+∙ Count files touched
+∙ Weight changes by complexity, not just line count
+∙ Compare against configurable thresholds
+∙ Consider test code separately from implementation
+∙ Account for generated or boilerplate code
+∙ Output a size classification: too small, right, too big
+∙ Provide reasoning for the classification
+
+Scope Comparison
+Compare two diffs or approaches to detect drift, additions, or omissions between them.
+∙ Normalize diffs for comparison
+∙ Identify lines present in one but not the other
+∙ Detect semantic equivalence despite textual differences
+∙ Flag behavioral changes vs cosmetic changes
+∙ Measure coverage overlap percentage
+∙ Highlight specific drift locations
+∙ Distinguish intentional refinement from accidental drift
+∙ Output a detailed comparison report
+
+PR Composition
+Write PR titles, descriptions, and commit messages following conventions and best practices.
+∙ Generate titles in imperative mood under 50 characters
+∙ Structure descriptions with what, why, and how sections
+∙ Write commit messages that explain the change
+∙ Include references to related issues or PRs
+∙ Format code blocks and lists in markdown
+∙ Adapt tone to match repository norms
+∙ Suggest labels based on change type
+∙ Output complete PR metadata ready for submission
+
+Repo Awareness
+Read and understand repository conventions, ownership, and contribution requirements.
+∙ Parse CODEOWNERS for reviewer suggestions
+∙ Read CONTRIBUTING.md for submission guidelines
+∙ Detect code style from existing files or configs
+∙ Identify protected branches and merge requirements
+∙ Find related past PRs for context
+∙ Understand CI requirements from workflow files
+∙ Map teams to code areas
+∙ Output relevant context for other agents
+
+Rules
+These rule definitions will be passed to an expert who will encode each as a constraint or validation check that agents must respect during execution.
+
+One Theme Per PR
+A pull request must address exactly one logical concern, never bundling unrelated changes together.
+∙ Define “theme” as a single bugfix, feature, refactor, or dependency change
+∙ Reject PRs that fix a bug AND add a feature
+∙ Reject PRs that refactor AND change behavior
+∙ Allow necessary test additions alongside implementation
+∙ Allow minimal documentation updates for the change
+∙ Scoper agent enforces this rule
+∙ Provide clear feedback on what themes were detected
+∙ Suggest how to split when violated
+
+Preserve Intent
+The collection of split PRs must be functionally equivalent to the original diff, with nothing added or lost.
+∙ Combined patches should produce identical end state
+∙ No behavioral changes introduced during splitting
+∙ No behavioral changes removed during splitting
+∙ Glue code from Fixer doesn’t count as added behavior
+∙ Observer agent enforces this rule
+∙ Compare at the semantic level, not just text
+∙ Flag any deviation for human review
+∙ Track equivalence across the full split set
+
+Smallest Viable Change
+Extract the minimum code necessary to achieve the theme’s purpose, nothing more.
+∙ Include only files directly relevant to the theme
+∙ Include only hunks that implement the theme
+∙ Exclude opportunistic cleanups unrelated to theme
+∙ Exclude “while I’m here” fixes
+∙ Extractor agent follows this rule
+∙ Scoper agent validates it
+∙ Prefer too small over too large
+∙ Err toward splitting further when uncertain
+
+Independence First
+Each split PR should ideally be mergeable without requiring the other split PRs to land first.
+∙ Avoid creating chains of dependent PRs when possible
+∙ Use stubs or mocks to break dependencies
+∙ Accept some duplication to achieve independence
+∙ Flag unavoidable dependencies for Sequencer
+∙ Independent PRs can be reviewed in parallel
+∙ Independent PRs survive if siblings are rejected
+∙ Extractor and Fixer work toward this goal
+∙ Sequencer handles cases where it’s not achievable
+
+Refactors Before Features
+Structural improvements must be submitted before new functionality that builds on them.
+∙ Identify refactors: renames, extractions, reorganization
+∙ Identify features: new behavior, new capabilities
+∙ Sequencer places refactors earlier in the order
+∙ Features reference refactor PRs as dependencies
+∙ This maximizes refactor merge likelihood
+∙ Reviewers prefer reviewing clean structure first
+∙ Reduces cognitive load on feature review
+∙ Prevents feature rejection from blocking refactor
+
+Deps First
+Dependency updates must be submitted before code that relies on the updated dependencies.
+∙ Identify dependency changes: Gemfile, package.json, etc.
+∙ Identify code using newly available APIs
+∙ Sequencer places dep updates at the front
+∙ Minimal dep-only PRs are easy to review
+∙ Reduces risk of breaking main with partial changes
+∙ Allows CI to validate dep compatibility first
+∙ Code PRs can assume deps are already merged
+∙ Clear separation of concerns for reviewers
+
+No Scope Creep
+The Fixer agent may only add glue code to make patches work, never expanding what the PR does.
+∙ Glue code: imports, type stubs, minimal mocks
+∙ Not glue code: new functions, new tests, new features
+∙ Fixer cannot fix bugs it notices along the way
+∙ Fixer cannot improve code quality beyond the theme
+∙ Document any tempting improvements for future PRs
+∙ Scoper validates Fixer didn’t expand scope
+∙ Observer catches scope creep in final comparison
+∙ Human review required if glue code seems substantial
+
+Description Matches Code
+The PR description must accurately reflect what the code changes actually do, no more and no less.
+∙ Every claim in description must be supported by code
+∙ Every significant code change must be mentioned
+∙ Title must summarize the actual change
+∙ Aligner agent enforces this rule
+∙ Reject descriptions that oversell
+∙ Reject descriptions that undersell
+∙ Commit messages must match their diffs
+∙ Update descriptions when code changes during review
+
+Respect Upstream Style
+Follow the target repository’s conventions for code style, commit format, and PR structure.
+∙ Read and apply existing linter configuration
+∙ Match naming conventions from surrounding code
+∙ Follow commit message format from repo history
+∙ Use PR template if one exists
+∙ Repo Awareness skill provides this context
+∙ Writer agent applies it to PR composition
+∙ Fixer agent applies it to code changes
+∙ Don’t impose personal preferences on upstream
+
+Flag Uncertainty
+When any agent is unsure about a decision, surface it for human review rather than guessing.
+∙ Define confidence thresholds for each decision type
+∙ Low confidence on theme assignment: flag it
+∙ Low confidence on hunk splitting: flag it
+∙ Low confidence on scope judgment: flag it
+∙ Collect flags into a human review queue
+∙ Provide context for why uncertainty exists
+∙ Continue processing other parts if possible
+∙ Never ship a PR with unresolved flags
+
+Atomic Commits
+Each commit within a PR should represent a single logical step that makes sense on its own.
+∙ One commit per logical change within the theme
+∙ Commits should compile individually if possible
+∙ Commit messages explain that step’s purpose
+∙ Don’t squash everything into one commit
+∙ Don’t have dozens of tiny fixup commits
+∙ Writer agent structures commits appropriately
+∙ Rebase and reorganize if needed
+∙ History should tell a readable story
+
+Traceable Lineage
+Every split PR must reference its origin so reviewers understand the context and can find related PRs.
+∙ Include link to original source PR or branch
+∙ Include link to sibling split PRs
+∙ Note which theme number this is (e.g., 3 of 7)
+∙ Explain the splitting rationale briefly
+∙ Writer agent adds these references
+∙ Helps reviewers see the big picture
+∙ Enables tracking split PR outcomes
+∙ Supports post-merge archaeology​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​
